---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import R from '../../components/R.astro';
import LevelStrip from '../../components/LevelStrip.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/paths';
import { ROUTES } from '../../lib/i18n';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to build an English immersion at home | englishmap"
  description="How to switch your surroundings to English, what to listen to and read at each level, and a sample day with about two hours of English."
  lang="en"
  route="guide"
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>How to build an English immersion at home</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      People who move abroad learn because they spend the day listening, reading and answering in
      English, almost always about things they can work out from context. The plan below tries to
      recreate that with what you already have at home: your phone, your computer, your headphones
      and a few conversations a week.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it works</h2>
    <p>
      It rests on Stephen Krashen’s idea of <em>comprehensible input</em>: we acquire a language
      when we understand messages in it, ideally a little above our current level. Studying grammar
      helps you organise what you know, but what lets English come out without translating in your
      head is a lot of exposure to things you understand and want to understand.
    </p>
    <p>
      So immersion here does not mean moving to another country. It means spending as many hours as
      possible doing in English what you would do anyway: watching videos, listening to something on
      the commute, reading about your favourite subject, talking.
    </p>

    <h2>Start with your surroundings</h2>
    <p>One afternoon of setup is worth weeks of willpower.</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Phone and computer in English.</strong> The first week feels odd. After that you
        stop noticing, and the menus you already know by heart turn into vocabulary.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Streaming profiles in English, with English subtitles.</strong> Subtitles in your
        own language make you read instead of listen. The <R id="language-reactor" /> extension
        shows both when you need them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Social media about what you already follow.</strong> Follow English-language
        accounts on football, cooking, programming, make-up, whatever you like. The algorithm does
        the rest.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Games with English text and voices.</strong> You read and listen without noticing
        that you are studying.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An English-English dictionary instead of a translator.</strong> The{' '}
        <R id="cambridge-dictionary" /> explains a word with others you already know and shows its
        level.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Talk to yourself.</strong> Describe out loud what you are doing while you cook or
        tidy up. When a word is missing, note it down and look it up later.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Full attention and background sound</h2>
    <p>
      Watching an episode with full attention, replaying bits and looking up words is active
      immersion. Leaving a podcast on while you wash the dishes is passive. Both count, but active
      immersion gives far more per hour. Use passive listening to fill time you would lose anyway,
      ideally with things you have already watched closely.
    </p>
    <p>
      A simple rule for choosing material: if you have to pause after every sentence, it is too hard
      and you should step down a level. If you understand everything effortlessly, step up.
    </p>

    <h2>Techniques that help</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Shadowing.</strong> Listen to a short sentence and say it along with the speaker,
        copying rhythm and intonation, without pausing. It works well with episodes you already
        understand, such as <R id="6-minute-english" />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Music.</strong> Lyrics repeat structures and stick in your head. On{' '}
        <R id="lingoclip" /> you fill in the lyrics as you listen.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Talk to an AI before you talk to people.</strong> Apps such as <R id="praktika" />{' '}
        let you practise without feeling shy. Take the mistakes you keep making to a real
        conversation on <R id="tandem" /> or to a lesson.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>English for your field.</strong> If you work in tech, business or security, the
        podcasts in the <a href={url('/en/#trabalho')}>work</a> and{' '}
        <a href={url('/en/#tecnologia')}>hack</a> sections teach the vocabulary you will use in
        meetings and interviews.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What to use at each stage</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Stage</th>
            <th scope="col">Listen and watch</th>
            <th scope="col">Read</th>
            <th scope="col">Speak and write</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              Beginning
              <LevelStrip from="A1" to="A2" lang="en" />
            </th>
            <td>
              <R id="peppa-pig" />, <R id="voa-learning-english" /> and <R id="easy-stories" />.
              Pictures and repetition help more than speed.
            </td>
            <td>
              Level 1 of <R id="news-in-levels" /> and the lowest levels of{' '}
              <R id="breaking-news-english" />.
            </td>
            <td>
              Repeat sentences out loud. A short course such as <R id="usa-learns" /> gives you the
              basic structure.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              Middle
              <LevelStrip from="B1" to="B2" lang="en" />
            </th>
            <td>
              <R id="6-minute-english" />, <R id="easy-english" />, <R id="all-ears-english" /> and
              series with English subtitles.
            </td>
            <td>
              <R id="simple-wikipedia" />, <R id="lingq" /> and one <R id="penguin-readers" /> book
              a month.
            </td>
            <td>
              A conversation every week on <R id="tandem" /> or <R id="free4talk" /> and a paragraph
              a day on <R id="langcorrect" />.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              Fluency
              <LevelStrip from="C1" to="C2" lang="en" />
            </th>
            <td>
              <R id="this-american-life" />, <R id="ted" />, <R id="kurzgesagt" /> and series
              without subtitles.
            </td>
            <td>
              <R id="the-guardian" /> and unabridged books from <R id="standard-ebooks" />.
            </td>
            <td>
              Free conversation on hard topics, long-form writing and fine-tuning pronunciation with{' '}
              <R id="youglish" />.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>A sample day</h2>
    <p>
      For someone at B1 who works away from home all day. That is about two hours of English, almost
      all of it fitted into moments that are already part of the routine.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="day">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">When</th>
            <th scope="col">Time</th>
            <th scope="col">What</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Getting ready</th>
            <td>15 min</td>
            <td>
              An episode of <R id="6-minute-english" />.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Commute</th>
            <td>30 min</td>
            <td>
              <R id="all-ears-english" />, or an episode you have heard before, as review.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Lunch</th>
            <td>15 min</td>
            <td>
              One story from <R id="breaking-news-english" /> at your level.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Late afternoon</th>
            <td>20 min</td>
            <td>
              A conversation on <R id="tandem" /> or in a <R id="free4talk" /> room, three times a
              week.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Evening</th>
            <td>45 min</td>
            <td>
              An episode of a series with English subtitles and <R id="language-reactor" />.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Before bed</th>
            <td>10 min</td>
            <td>
              Three sentences on <R id="langcorrect" /> and the day’s review in <R id="anki" />.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>How to tell it is working</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Log your hours per day in a notebook or spreadsheet. The running total is what tracks
        progress most closely, and watching it grow helps keep the habit.
      </li>
      <li>
        Take the <R id="ef-set" /> now and again every three months. Compare the reading and
        listening scores separately.
      </li>
      <li>
        Re-read or re-watch something that was hard three months ago. Noticing it has become easy is
        the best measure.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Further reading</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li>
        Stephen Krashen,{' '}
        <a href="https://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/principles_and_practice.pdf">
          Principles and Practice in Second Language Acquisition
        </a>{' '}
        (1982). The book that set out the input hypothesis.
      </li>
      <li>
        Yo Hamada,{' '}
        <a href="https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0033688218771380">
          Shadowing: What is it? How to use it. Where will it go?
        </a>{' '}
        (RELC Journal, 2019). A review of the shadowing technique.
      </li>
      <li>
        Refold,{' '}
        <a href="https://refold.la/roadmap/phase-0/0c-immerse-in-the-language">
          Immerse in the language
        </a>
        . A language-learning roadmap built around immersion.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="back">
      <a href={url(ROUTES.home.en)}>See all resources</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: calc(var(--measure) + 2 * var(--space-6));
    padding-block: var(--space-7) 0;
    font-size: var(--type-prose);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-entry);
    font-weight: var(--weight-entry);
    font-size: var(--type-h1);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .lead {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    font-size: var(--type-h3);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-entry);
    font-weight: var(--weight-entry);
    font-size: var(--type-h2);
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  p + p {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  ol,
  ul {
    padding-inline-start: 1.25em;
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
  }

  li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  li::marker {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .table-scroll {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 36rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--type-data);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-3) var(--space-3) 0;
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  thead th {
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    border-bottom-color: var(--rule-strong);
  }

  tbody th {
    font-weight: var(--weight-strong);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  tbody th :global(.level) {
    display: flex;
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
  }

  .day {
    min-width: 30rem;
  }

  .day td:nth-child(2) {
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .sources {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .back {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
  }
</style>
